/-!
# The conductor formula for the normalization of a scheme with double crossings in codimension one

Let `X` be an `S₂` complex `n`-fold with at worst double normal crossings in codimension one,
`ν : X^ν ⟶ X` its normalization, `Δ` a `ℚ`-Weil divisor on `X` and `Θ = ν⁻¹_*Δ + D^ν` the boundary
of the normalization. Let `ω_X = H^{-n}(ω_X^•)` and `ω_{X^ν} = H^{-n}(ω_{X^ν}^•)` be the dualizing
sheaves, cut out of the dualizing complexes normalized relative to `ℂ`, and let
`Tr_ν : ν_*ω_{X^ν} ⟶ ω_X` be the trace of the finite morphism `ν`. Let `K_X = div(s)` for a
rational section `s` of `ω_X` generating it at the generic points and at the double-crossing
points of codimension one, and `K_{X^ν} = div(ν^*s)`, where `ν^*s` is the rational section of
`ω_{X^ν}` with `Tr_ν(ν^*s) = s`. If `L` is the Cartier divisor `m(K_X + Δ)` defined by `s^m`, then
`ν^*L` exists and its Weil divisor is `m(K_{X^ν} + Θ)`.

The comparison is made one prime divisor `Γ'` of `X^ν` at a time.

* Off the conductor, `ν` is an isomorphism near `Γ'` and `Tr_ν` an isomorphism there, so all
  orders along `Γ'` are those along its image.
* Along a conductor component over `P`, the stalk `𝒪_{X,P}` is seminormal, being a localization
  of the stalk at a closed double normal crossing point, whose completion
  `k[[x₀, …, x_{m+1}]]/(x₀x₁)` is seminormal; seminormality descends by faithful flatness. In a
  seminormal ring the conductor is radical, and by duality it is generated by the coefficient
  `θ` of `Tr_ν` on a local generator of `ω_{X^ν}`. So `θ` is a uniformizer at every point over
  `P`, `ν^*s` has a simple pole along `Γ'`, and `K_{X^ν} + Θ` has coefficient `0` there, as does
  `ν^*L`.

The file proceeds in that order: seminormality of double crossings and its localization, the
conductor of a seminormal finite extension, the trace on local generators, the pole along the
conductor, dimension theory for the finite morphism `ν`, the comparison off the conductor, and
finally finite duality for the trace, from which the theorem is assembled.

## Main results

* `MMP.Flips.IsBoundaryOfNormalization.isWeilDivisorOfCartier_pullback`: the conductor formula
  `m(K_{X^ν} + Θ) = ν^*(m(K_X + Δ))`.

## Implementation notes

The dualizing sheaves are `ω_X = H^0(ω_X^•[-n])` and `ω_{X^ν} = H^0(ω_{X^ν}^•[-n])` for the
normalized dualizing complexes `MMP.Flips.normalizedDualizingComplexOverField`. The trace is the
canonical one: `ν` is finite, hence proper, the twisted inverse image `a` of `ν` (right adjoint of
`Rν_*`) carries `ω_X^•` to `ω_{X^ν}^•` ([Stacks, Tag 0AUD]), and `Tr_ν` is `H^0` of the counit
`Rν_* a(ω_X^•[-n]) ⟶ ω_X^•[-n]` (`Stacks.Duality.FiniteTrace.trace`), which needs `a(ω_X^•[-n])`
to be concentrated in degrees `≥ 0`; that amplitude bound is part of the conclusion. The pull-back
`ν^*s` is a section `t` of `ω_{X^ν}` over `ν⁻¹W'` with `Tr_ν(t) = s|_{W'}`, for an open `W'`
containing the generic points of `X`. The Cartier divisor `L` is required to be the divisor of
`s^m` at the generic points and the codimension-one double points.

Duality for `ν` is a theorem here: over every affine open `V ⊆ X`, every `𝒪_X(V)`-linear map
`𝒪_{X^ν}(ν⁻¹V) ⟶ ω_X(V)` is `a ↦ Tr_ν(a · f)` for a unique `f ∈ ω_{X^ν}(ν⁻¹V)`, and a
compactification of `X` (Nagata) shows that `ω_X` and `ω_{X^ν}` are dualizing sheaves in the
sense of `MMP.Flips.IsDualizingSheaf`.

`ℚ`-Weil divisors on `X` are supported on prime divisors with discrete valuation rings as local
rings (`MMP.Flips.QWeilDivisor`); a component of the double locus has a non-regular local ring,
so `Δ` and `K_X` have no double-locus component by construction. On a non-normal `X` a Cartier
divisor is not determined by its Weil divisor along the double locus: on `{xy = 0} ⊆ 𝔸³`,
`div((x + y²)/(x² + y))` has Weil divisor zero but pulls back to `Q₂ - Q₁` on the two branches
over the double line. This is why `L` is required to have trivial germ at the double points
(`MMP.Intersection.cartierSupport` avoids them).

## References

* [J. Kollár et al., *Flips and abundance for algebraic threefolds*][KollarFlips], 12.2.2, 16.3
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AUD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AUD)
-/

